# Text-only build of Qwen3.5-27B for code mutation.
#
# Identical to Modelfile.qwen35-27b-code except that it names the weights blob
# directly instead of inheriting the packaged model, which drops the 0.87 GB
# CLIP vision projector. OpenEvolve mutates source code and never sends an
# image, so that projector is memory spent on a capability this workload cannot
# use -- and on a box where only ~32 of 65 layers fit on the GPU, 0.87 GB is
# roughly four more layers that stop running on the CPU.
#
# RENDERER/PARSER are restated because naming a blob directly does not inherit
# them, and without them the chat template is wrong in ways that look like the
# model being bad at instructions.

FROM C:\Users\vardh\.ollama\models\blobs\sha256-ce18b852ff0f7f7fc3cbe3467b4a87d3b27e7b3e611bf41e0a529220604aa79f
TEMPLATE {{ .Prompt }}
RENDERER qwen3.5
PARSER qwen3.5

PARAMETER temperature 0.7
PARAMETER top_p 0.8
PARAMETER top_k 20
PARAMETER min_p 0
PARAMETER repeat_penalty 1.05
PARAMETER num_ctx 8192

# All twelve logical cores, not the six physical ones Ollama defaults to.
#
# The usual advice is that hyperthreads hurt, because two logical cores share a
# physical core's vector units. Measured here it is the other way round, and
# monotonically:
#
#     num_thread  4    64.6 prompt/s   3.14 gen/s
#     num_thread  6    63.8            3.26      <- Ollama's default
#     num_thread  8    69.6            3.38
#     num_thread 12    72.4            3.41
#
# +4.6% generation and +13% prompt over the default. Modest, and free. The
# advice is written for workloads that saturate the vector units; ~60% of this
# model runs on the CPU and is memory-bandwidth bound, where a stalled thread
# leaves units idle for its sibling to use.
PARAMETER num_thread 12

# DO NOT add `PARAMETER num_gpu`. Measured on this box, forcing it is
# monotonically worse -- every step up costs throughput:
#
#     auto (39% resident)   3.26 tok/s      <-- leave it alone
#     num_gpu 36 (54%)      2.56 tok/s      -21%
#     num_gpu 40 (60%)      2.08 tok/s      -36%
#     num_gpu 44            1.65 tok/s      -49%
#     num_gpu 48            1.39 tok/s      -57%
#
# Prompt evaluation collapses with it, 68.9 -> ~20 tok/s. The cause is that
# "more layers on the GPU" stops being true past what actually fits: Windows
# lets the driver spill into shared GPU memory, which is system RAM addressed
# over PCIe and slower than simply running those layers on the CPU. Ollama's
# automatic split already accounts for the KV cache and compute buffers.
#
# num_ctx 4096 measured identical to 8192 (3.25 vs 3.26 tok/s), so context is
# not the constraint here and there is nothing to buy by shrinking it.
PARAMETER stop "</think>"
PARAMETER stop "<|im_end|>"
